theory_blocks.body:
A **planetary nebula** is an expanding shell of gas shed by a low-mass star near the end of its life, leaving behind the exposed, hot core as a white dwarf.

The name comes only from how such nebulae first looked through early, low-resolution telescopes: round and planet-like in outline. A planetary nebula contains no planet, and is not itself becoming a planet;

it is simply the star's own outer layers, now drifting outward and glowing as they disperse into space.

This lesson climbs through four nested levels, each one far larger than the last: the **Sun**, one ordinary star; the **Milky Way**, the galaxy containing the Sun along with billions of other stars, all bound together by their mutual gravity;

**galaxies** in general, of which the Milky Way is just one among many billions; and the **Universe**, which contains all of these galaxies and everything in them.

[[blue:Each level is genuinely inside the next]]: the Sun is inside the Milky Way, the Milky Way is one galaxy among the billions that make up the observed Universe. None of these levels replaces or stands apart from the others; they are nested, like the planet-moon-Sun structure from earlier in this unit, but at a vastly larger scale.

